**Considering MCMC Techniques**

I need to look into boundary sampling using reflections to preserve Gaussian symmetry, but I'm wondering if non-diagonal covariance breaks reversibility. For correlated Gaussians that aren’t symmetric at the boundary, I can reject invalid samples. High-dimensional posteriors can produce high rejection rates, so I’ll need to adaptively shrink. Using hit-and-run slice MCMC from covariance eigen directions could be efficient. Coordinate Gibbs methods with local linear Hessians might work here too. I'll think about using differential evolution for an ensemble approach as well.
